@page "/legal/privacy"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Privacy · GymStation</PageTitle>

<main class="legal-shell">
    <header>
        <a class="legal-home" href="/">GYMSTATION</a>
        <h1>PRIVACY</h1>
        <p class="legal-date">EFFECTIVE 2026-08-02</p>
    </header>

    <p>
        GymStation is a platform gyms use to run their day-to-day: schedules, rosters,
        attendance, ranks, and dues. Each gym operates its own space here. This page
        explains what the platform stores and who can see it.
    </p>

    <h2>WHAT WE STORE</h2>
    <p><b>Your account.</b> Your email address, a hashed password (we never store the
        password itself), your theme preference, and which gym you last had active.</p>
    <p><b>Your gym record.</b> What your gym enters or you provide: name, date of birth
        (optional), belt and rank history, contact number and SMS preference, attendance,
        and your membership and payment ledger with that gym.</p>
    <p><b>Your training records.</b> Diary entries, logged classes, and progress notes
        you write yourself.</p>
    <p><b>Gym content.</b> Logos, photos, schedules, and event listings a gym publishes
        for its public page.</p>

    <h2>WHO SEES WHAT</h2>
    <p>Your gym's staff can see the roster record, attendance, and ledger for their own
        gym — that's the bookkeeping you'd expect the front desk to have.</p>
    <p>Your training diary is different: it is private to your account authority — you,
        or a guardian managing a ward's account. Gym staff can never read it.</p>
    <p>Gyms are separate spaces. One gym cannot see anything about another gym or its
        members. If you train at several gyms, each sees only its own record of you.</p>
    <p>A gym's public page shows only what that gym chooses to publish: its schedule,
        events, and instructor bios. Member information never appears there.</p>

    <h2>WHAT WE DON'T DO</h2>
    <p>No advertising, no analytics trackers, no third-party scripts, and no sale or
        sharing of data with anyone. The platform sets
        <a href="/legal/cookies">two essential cookies</a> and nothing else.</p>

    <h2>CORRECTIONS AND REMOVAL</h2>
    <p>Your gym controls its roster records. To correct your record, remove your number,
        or close out your membership data, ask your gym's staff — they have the tools.
        Your diary is yours; you can edit or delete entries at any time.</p>

    <p class="legal-note">Questions about this policy go to your gym, who can raise them
        with the platform operator.</p>

    <PlatformFooter />
</main>
